<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paracel Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hoang Sa, Xisha Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Paracel Islands are a group of small islands and reefs in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> took the western part of the group from South Vietnam in a naval battle in January 1974 and has controlled all of them since. Vietnam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> continue to claim them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paracel-islands.jpg" alt="Paracel Islands" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States Naval Oceanographic Office · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asia_-_South_China_Sea_-_Mui_Da_Nang_to_Shen-Ch%27uan_Chiang_including_Hai-Nan_Tao_and_Paracel_Islands_(Paracel_Islands_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The archipelago includes about 130 small coral islands and reefs, most of them grouped into the northeastern Amphitrite Group or the western Crescent Group. They are spread over a maritime area of around 15,000 square kilometres, with a land area of only about 7.75 square kilometres. The highest point, an unnamed location on Rocky Island, is 14 m above sea level. The islands lie about 350 km southeast of Hainan Island, equidistant from the coastlines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. The climate is tropical, with abundant rainfall and annual typhoons, and the surrounding waters are productive fishing grounds.</p><p>The Amphitrite Group was named after the French frigate Amphitrite, which observed the islands while carrying a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> in 1698–1700. Woody Island, the largest of the Paracels at 213 hectares, lies at its centre. The islands of the Crescent Group are named after senior figures of the British East India Company, among them James Drummond, Thomas Pattle, John William Roberts and Jonathan Duncan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The word paracel is of Portuguese origin and probably comes from the Spanish placer, used by navigators for shallow sandy seas or submerged banks. Portuguese maps of the early 16th century marked a group of flooded islands in the area, and from the mid-1500s the group was called Ilhas do Pracel. Chinese works from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> onward described large groups of maritime features such as Qianli Changsha and Wanli Shitang, and Xisha, meaning Western Sands, became the standard Chinese name in the 20th century. The Vietnamese name Hoàng Sa, Yellow Sands, appears in documents dating back to 1483.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> all claim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and each presents its own historical case. Chinese accounts date an interest to the second century BCE; Vietnamese accounts point to the planting of a flag in 1816 and ten inscribed steles installed in 1836. Between 1881 and 1883 the German navy surveyed the islands each year without seeking permission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or China, and published the results in 1885. China has controlled the whole archipelago since the Battle of the Paracel Islands in January 1974.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paracel Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thiệu declared on 4 January 1974 that the war had restarted, and the same month China took the <b>Paracel Islands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Tensions with China over the <b>Paracel</b> and Spratly Islands periodically flare.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
